#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:22
msgid "Polyconic projection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:24
msgid ""
"The polyconic projection, in Europe usually referred to as the American "
"polyconic projection, was introduced shortly before 1820 by the Swiss-"
"American cartographer Ferdinand Rodulph Hassler (1770-1843). As head of the "
"Survey of the Coast, he was looking for a projection that would give the "
"least distortion for mapping the coast of the United States. The projection "
"acquired its name from the construction of each parallel, which is achieved "
"by projecting the parallel onto the cone while it is rolled around the "
"globe, along the central meridian, tangent to that parallel. As a "
"consequence, the projection involves many cones rather than a single one "
"used in regular conic projections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:34
msgid ""
"The polyconic projection is neither equal-area, nor conformal. It is true to "
"scale without distortion along the central meridian. Each parallel is true "
"to scale as well, but the meridians are not as they get further away from "
"the central meridian. As a consequence, no parallel is standard because "
"conformity is lost with the lengthening of the meridians."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:40
msgid ""
"**poly**/\\ [*lon0*/\\ [*lat0*/]]\\ *scale* or **Poly**/\\ [*lon0*/\\ "
"[*lat0*/]]\\ *width*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:43
msgid "**poly** or **Poly**: Sets the projection type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:44
msgid "*lon0*: Sets the central meridian [Optional]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:45
msgid ""
"*lat0*: Sets the reference parallel [Optional]. When supplied, *lon0* must "
"be supplied as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:47
msgid "*scale* or *width*: Sets the map size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projections/conic/polyconic.rst:51
msgid ""
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Axis\n"
"\n"
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-180, -20, 0, 90],\n"
"    projection=\"Poly/12c\",\n"
"    frame=Axis(annot=True, tick=True, grid=True),\n"
"    land=\"seagreen\",\n"
"    water=\"gray90\",\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

